  "summary": "A fortified village made of mudbrick common in the oases of the Sahara.",
  "description": "Features compact, high-walled structures with communal living spaces and narrow winding streets designed for defense against raiders while providing thermal mass in extreme desert conditions.",
  "culturalContext": "The ksour (plural ksar) — fortified mudbrick villages of Morocco's Drâa and Ziz valleys and southern Algeria — stack family towers (igherman) within defensive walls around palm-oasis economy: Aït Benhaddou (UNESCO, film-starred in Gladiator and Game of Thrones), Tinghir, Ghardaïa's M'zab pentad. Collective granaries (agadir), sumptuous gates and earthen repair cycles made ksar life communitarian; rural exodus emptied many, and earthen-architecture conservation programs now train artisans in the original mud-lime craft.",
